      "answer": "$6+\\frac12\\ln 10-2\\tan^{-1}3$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "B1: Split the fraction as $1+\\frac{x-4}{x^2+4}$. M1: Attempt integration and obtain $p\\ln(x^2+4)$ or $q\\tan^{-1}(x/2)$ from correct working. A1FT: Obtain $\\frac12\\ln(x^2+4)$, following their coefficients after a correct split; the listed equivalent split is allowed. A1FT: Obtain $-2\\tan^{-1}(x/2)$, only from a correct split. M1: Use limits $0$ and $6$ correctly in an expression involving $p\\ln(x^2+4)$ and $q\\tan^{-1}(x/2)$ with no incorrect terms; $p,q$ are constants and the $x$ term need not yet be shown. A1 ISW: Obtain $6+\\frac12\\ln10-2\\tan^{-1}3$, or a three-term equivalent with the logarithms combined. Alternative: B1 substitute $x=2\\tan\\theta$ to obtain $\\int(2\\tan^2\\theta+\\tan\\theta)\\,d\\theta$. M1 attempt integration and obtain $p\\tan\\theta$ or $r\\ln(\\sec\\theta)$. A1FT obtain $2\\tan\\theta-2\\theta$. A1FT obtain $\\ln(\\sec\\theta)$. M1 use limits $0$ and $\\tan^{-1}3$ correctly. A1 ISW obtain $6-2\\tan^{-1}3+\\ln(\\sec(\\tan^{-1}3))$; it need not be simplified.\nTotal: [6]",
